1
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:13,720
The world of sport is full of wonderful moments.

2
00:00:14,540 --> 00:00:16,347
Here are our top three.

3
00:00:19,980 --> 00:00:21,896
The starting gun went off.

4
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:28,920
Suddenly, eight athletes were flying down the track in Rio de Janeiro’s Olympic Stadium.

5
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:33,146
Less than 20 seconds later the race was over.

6
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:43,020
Usain Bolt was walking around the stadium with the Jamaican flag over his shoulders – he was a double Olympic champion … again!

7
00:00:44,320 --> 00:01:02,920
Four years earlier, at the 2012 London Olympics, he became the first man to win a gold medal in both the 100m and 200m sprint at a second Olympic Games (he won them both at the 2008 Beijing Olympics, too).

8
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:06,640
In Rio, he did it for a third time.

9
00:01:07,860 --> 00:01:23,540
As well as his eight Olympic gold medals (he won the other two in relay races with other teammates), Bolt also won 11 world championships and currently holds the world record for the 100m and 200m.

10
00:01:24,980 --> 00:01:27,840
He is one of the greatest sprinters in history.

11
00:01:32,060 --> 00:01:43,584
Everyone was looking at 15-year-old Romanian gymnast Nadia Comaneci as she left the bars, flew through the air and landed perfectly on the ground.

12
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:45,320
She turned round.

13
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:47,360
The crowd was cheering.

14
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:52,083
Everyone was waiting nervously for the judges’ score.

15
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:53,480
Then it came.

16
00:01:54,560 --> 00:01:57,340
The scoreboard was showing ‘1.00’.

17
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:01,600
The crowd was confused.

18
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:04,680
But then the organisers explained.

19
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:11,140
The makers of the scoreboards thought it was impossible to get a perfect score of 10.

20
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:15,100
The boards were not able to show ‘10.00’.

21
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:29,548
So, on 18 July 1976, at the Montreal Olympics, Nadia made history when she became the first gymnast ever to get a perfect ten.

22
00:02:30,140 --> 00:02:40,670
She got six more ‘perfect scores’ at the games and won three gold medals, making her one of the best athletes in her sport of all time.

23
00:02:45,180 --> 00:02:46,603
It was the final lap.

24
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:55,100
Roger Bannister was running fast, but could he really become the first person to run a mile in under four minutes?

25
00:02:56,260 --> 00:03:00,407
A minute later, he was lying exhausted on the ground.

26
00:03:01,460 --> 00:03:08,600
Then, there was an announcement of his time: ‘3 minutes, 59.4 seconds.’

27
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:11,160
The crowd went crazy.

28
00:03:12,840 --> 00:03:20,667
Before 6 May 1954, experts said that a four-minute mile was impossible to run.

29
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:23,778
Roger showed them that they were wrong.

30
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:32,428
He planned to stop running after the 1952 Summer Olympics, but there he only came in fourth place.

31
00:03:32,428 --> 00:03:37,520
Without a medal, Roger wanted to show how good he was.

32
00:03:38,460 --> 00:03:41,840
In 1954, he did exactly that.
